{"results":[{"url":"https://www.rockyhillct.gov/DocumentCenter/View/2777/Automatic-License-Plate-Reader-ALPR-Policy","final_url":"https://www.rockyhillct.gov/DocumentCenter/View/2777/Automatic-License-Plate-Reader-ALPR-Policy","title":null,"description":null,"language":null,"text":"Rocky Hill Police Department \n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \n \nBrian P. Klett \nChief of Police \n \n \nROCKY HILL POLICE DEPARTMENT \nGENERAL ORDER \nCHAPTER  61 \nNUMBER \n61-1 (a) \nTITLE: AUTOMATIC LICENSE PLATE READERS (ALPR) \nEFFECTIVE: 08/5/14 ISSUED: B.P.K. REVIEW:  \nREVISED: 10/1/26 \nRESCINDS:  \nCALEA REFERENCE: 41.3.9 \nSTATE REFERENCE:  \n \nPURPOSE \n \nThe purpose of this policy is to establish requirements and safeguards governing the \noperation and use of Automated License Plate Recognition (ALPR) systems and access \nto ALPR data by the Rocky Hill Police Department. \n \nThis policy establishes standards for ALPR system operation, data collection, access, \nquerying, sharing, retention, security, auditing, training, vendor management, \ntransparency, and accountability. \n \nThe Department recognizes that ALPR technology can assist legitimate law \nenforcement and public safety functions while also creating significant privacy and civil-\nliberty concerns because the technology may collect information concerning vehicles \ntraveling in public places. \n \nALPR technology shall therefore be used only for authorized purposes and in \naccordance with applicable federal law, Connecticut law, applicable regulations, the \ncurrent Connecticut Police Officer Standards and Training Council (POSTC) Model \nPolicy, and this Department policy.\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \nPOLICY \n \nIt is the policy of the Rocky Hill Police Department to use ALPR technology only for \nlegitimate law enforcement and authorized public safety purposes. \n \nALPR systems and ALPR data shall not be used for personal purposes, harassment, \nintimidation, retaliation, discrimination, investigation of constitutionally protected activity, \nprohibited immigration enforcement, prohibited investigations involving lawful \nreproductive or gender-affirming health care, or any other purpose prohibited by \nConnecticut or federal law. \n \nAll personnel authorized to use ALPR technology shall comply with this policy and shall \ncomplete required initial and periodic training before obtaining or continuing access. \n \nALPR OVERVIEW \n \nALPR technology uses cameras and software to capture images of vehicle license \nplates and, depending upon the system, vehicle characteristics such as make, model, \ncolor, and other identifying information. \n \nThe system may convert captured information into computer-readable data and \ncompare that information against authorized databases or lists. \n \nALPR technology may assist with authorized purposes including: \n \n• Identifying stolen or wanted vehicles; \n \n• Identifying stolen license plates; \n \n• Locating missing or endangered persons; \n \n• Locating persons with outstanding warrants; \n \n• Recovering stolen property or stolen vehicles; \n \n• Assisting specific active criminal investigations; \n \n• Assisting in circumstances involving flight or evasion from law \nenforcement; \n \n• Responding to emergencies; \n \n• Identifying vehicles associated with authorized alerts or databases; and \n \n• Other purposes expressly permitted by Connecticut law.\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \nAn ALPR alert is not, standing alone, conclusive evidence that a vehicle, license plate, \nor person is wanted or otherwise involved in criminal activity. \n \nDEFINITIONS \n \na. ALPR: Automated License Recognition, also referred to as Automated  \nLicense Plate Reader. \n \nb. ALPR Administrator: The person designated by the Chief of Police to  \noversee the Department's ALPR program, including compliance, access, \ntraining, security, auditing, system management, reporting, and policy \nimplementation. \n \nc. ALPR Data: Data generated by or obtained through an ALPR system,  \nincluding captured license plate information, images, vehicle descriptors, \nlocation information, timestamps, metadata, alert information, scan files, and \nassociated records, as defined by applicable law. \n \nd. Automatic License Plate Recognition (ALPR) device: consists of cameras \nenclosed in housings connected to a computer system whether hardwired or \ncloud-based that is designed to automatically recognize and interpret the \ncharacters on vehicle license plates.  A LPR may be fixed or mobile (mounted \non a vehicle or trailer). \n \ne. ALPR Operator: A trained Department member or other authorized person  \npermitted to operate an ALPR system. \n \nf. ALPR System: ALPR cameras and associated hardware, software, \ndatabases, networks, and related technology used by the Department, \nincluding fixed and mobile systems. \n \ng. Alert/Hit: A visual, electronic, and/or auditory notification indicating that ALPR \n data may correspond to information contained in an authorized list or \ndatabase. \nAn alert does not establish that the information is current or that the vehicle is \nthe vehicle associated with the underlying record. \n \nh. Gender-Affirming Health Care Services: As defined in Conn. Gen. Statute \n52-571m. \n \ni. Hot List: A list of license plate numbers or other vehicle identifiers maintained  \nfor an authorized law enforcement purpose and used to generate ALPR \nalerts. \n \nj. Internal Hotlist Addendum: A hotlist that (A) is maintained for use only at \nspecific state facility or facilities for which the list was made and by the public\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \nagency that made such list, and (B) may contain registration numbers \ndisplayed on a license plate of a motor vehicle registered to a person who is \nidentified in the making of such list as a potential threat to a person in the \nstate facility, to the state facility or against the public agency operating the \nstate facility, for reasons that include a previous conviction of such person for \na misdemeanor or felony offense involving threats of physical violence or \nharassment against a person in the state facility, to the state facility or against \nthe public agency operating the state facility or conduct by a person that is \ndocumented and involves threats of or attempted violence, harassment or \nincitement of physical violence against a person in the state facility, to the \nstate facility or against the public agency operating the state facility, provided \nno such inclusion on an internal Hotlist addendum is based on an arrest for or \nconduct that is solely speech, conduct or association protected by article first \nof the Constitution of the state or the first amendment to the United States \nConstitution. \n \nk. Reproductive Health Care Services: As defined in Conn. Gen. Stat. 52a-\n571m.   \n \nl. LPR Notification: A message generated by an LPR system which notifies \nthe user that a vehicle is a potential match for a list, whether locally \ngenerated, created by neighboring jurisdictions, or based on NCIC records.  \nAn LPR notification does not represent conclusive confirmation that the \ninformation contained in the notification is accurate.  Sometimes referred to \nas a “hit”. \n \nm. Query: A search of ALPR data using specified criteria, including a license \nplate number, vehicle characteristics, date, time, location, or other authorized \nparameters. \n \nn. Query Log: A record of an ALPR data search or query, including information \nrequired by law or Department policy. \n \no. State Facility: As defined by the applicable POSTC Model Policy and \nConnecticut Law. \n \n \nThis policy applies to any equipment, service, platform, or network that performs \nALPR functions or provides the Department access to ALPR data, regardless of \nvendor or ownership. \n \nALPR ADMINISTRATOR \n \nThe Chief of Police shall designate an ALPR Administrator. \n \nThe ALPR Administrator shall:\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \n \n1. Ensure the ALPR system is used only for authorized Department business; \n \n2. Ensure compliance with applicable federal and Connecticut law, the current \nPOSTC Model Policy, and this policy; \n \n3. Authorize and manage user access; \n \n4. Issue or approve user credentials; \n \n5. Ensure required initial and periodic training is completed; \n \n6. Monitor system security and privacy safeguards; \n \n7. Monitor system operation and coordinate repairs; \n \n8. Review or coordinate review of requests for external sharing of ALPR data; \n \n9. Conduct, or cause to be conducted, ALPR audits at least monthly; \n \n10. Review system access and query logs; \n \n11. Review ALPR data retention activity, including the number of times data is \nretained beyond the ordinary retention period and the duration of such \nretention; \n \n12. Ensure audit deficiencies are documented and corrective action is taken; \n \n13. Maintain records required by law and Department policy; \n \n14. Ensure required annual ALPR usage reports are prepared and submitted; \n \n15. Ensure required transparency information is published; \n \n16. Identify and report policy or legal violations to the Chief of Police; \n \n17. Recommend necessary policy, training, or system changes; and \n \n18. Ensure changes in law, regulation, POSTC requirements, DESPP \nrequirements, or ALPR technology are incorporated into Department \nprocedures and training.\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \nAUTHORIZED USE \n \nALPR systems and ALPR data may be operated, accessed, queried, or used only for \npurposes authorized by Connecticut law and this policy. \n \nAuthorized law enforcement uses include, when applicable legal requirements are \nsatisfied: \n \na. Comparing ALPR information with an authorized hot list; \n \nb. Comparing information with authorized Connecticut criminal justice \ndatabases; \n \nc. Using ALPR data in connection with a specific active criminal investigation \nwhen reasonable suspicion exists that a criminal offense has been or is being \ncommitted and the required documentation is completed; \n \nd. Assisting in the apprehension of an individual with an outstanding warrant; \n \ne. Assisting in locating a missing or endangered individual; \n \nf. Assisting in the recovery of a stolen motor vehicle; \n \ng. Responding to circumstances otherwise authorized by Connecticut law; \n \nh. Using authorized state, federal, or national databases where the use is \npermitted by law and applicable policy; and \n \ni. Other purposes expressly authorized by Connecticut law. \n \nThe Department shall not use ALPR data merely because a person or vehicle is of \ngeneral interest to law enforcement. \n \nFor a specific criminal investigation, personnel shall document the factual basis for \naccessing the data and the associated case, complaint, or incident number as required \nby law. \n \nWhen a case, complaint, or incident number exists, the query shall be linked to an \nindependently verifiable Department record when practicable. \n \nQUERIES AND ACCESS \n \nAn authorized user shall: \n \n1. Log into the ALPR system using individually assigned credentials;\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \n2. Use the system only for an authorized purpose; \n \n3. Enter only the parameters necessary to conduct the authorized query; \n \n4. Document the reason for the query; \n \n5. Identify the applicable offense or authorized purpose when required; \n \n6. Enter the applicable case, complaint, incident, or other identifying number \nwhen required; \n \n7. Complete any additional CAD or report documentation required by this policy. \n \n \nQueries shall comply with any applicable reasonable-suspicion, probable-cause, \nwarrant, or other legal requirement. \n \nExigent access shall be identified at the time of access, justified in writing with a call for \nservice number and case report, and included in a supervisory review. \n \nUsers shall not conduct queries merely out of curiosity or for personal purposes. \n \nDEPARTMENT CASE QUERIES \n \nWhen an officer searches or conducts a query for an ALPR-related purpose involving a \nRocky Hill Police Department case, the officer shall document the reason for the query \nin the original case report whenever practicable. \n \nIf the original report has already been completed, the officer shall complete a \nsupplemental report to the original case documenting: \n \n• The date and time of the query; \n \n• The officer conducting the query; \n \n• The case or incident number; \n \n• The information or vehicle searched, when appropriate; \n \n• The reason and authorized law enforcement purpose for the query; and \n \n• Any relevant information obtained as a result of the query. \n \nThe documentation requirement applies regardless of whether the query produces \ninformation of investigative value.\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \nHOT LISTS \n \nAuthorized users may enter license plate numbers or other vehicle identifiers into an \nauthorized hot list only for a legitimate purpose permitted by law and this policy. \n \nBefore a manual hot-list entry is created: \n \n1. The entry shall be approved by an authorized supervisor when required; \n \n2. The offense or authorized purpose shall be documented; \n \n3. The vehicle description and officer information shall be documented when \napplicable; \n \n4. The entry shall have an appropriate expiration date; \n \n5. The license plate information shall be verified through appropriate law \nenforcement databases when practicable; and \n \n6. The entry shall be removed when the expiration period ends or when the \nunderlying purpose no longer exists. \n \nHot-list entries shall not be based solely upon constitutionally protected speech, \nassociation, assembly, petitioning activity, or other protected conduct.  Hot lists received \nfrom outside sources shall identify the source and be periodically verified and updated \nprior to being re-entered.   \n \nBOLO AND ALERT ENTRY PROCEDURES \n \nA. BOLO’s Received by Dispatch From Outside Agencies \n \nWhen the Rocky Hill Police Department receives a BOLO or other ALPR-related \nrequest from an outside law enforcement agency or other authorized outside agency \nand dispatch is requested or directed to enter the information into the Department's \nALPR system: \n \n• The dispatcher shall obtain approval from the Shift Commander before \nentering the BOLO into the ALPR system; \n \n• The dispatcher shall create a call for service number (CFS) in CAD under the \nREKOR ENTRY call type. \n \n• The CAD entry shall identify the source of the BOLO and provide sufficient \ninformation to identify the vehicle, license plate, or other subject of the alert. \n \n•   The dispatcher shall document the Shift Commander's approval.\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \n \n• When the underlying BOLO or request expires, is cancelled, or is otherwise \nno longer valid, the Department shall take reasonable steps to remove or \ndeactivate the corresponding ALPR alert. \n \nB. Officer-Initiated ALPR Entries Not Associated With a RHPD Call for Service \n \nWhen a Rocky Hill Police Department officer determines that an ALPR entry is \nnecessary and the entry is not associated with an existing call for service number in \nthe Town of Rocky Hill.   \n \n• The officer shall obtain approval from the Shift Commander before making the \nentry.   \n \n• Obtain a call for service number under REKOR ENTRY call type; and  \n \n• Then document the purpose of the entry in a police report.   \n \n• When practical a CAD entry should also be made to detail the reason for the \ndepartment alert. \n \n \nC. Department Alerts Associated With Rocky Hill Calls for Service \n \nWhen a Department ALPR alert is associated with an existing call for service in the \nTown of Rocky Hill, the officer shall obtain Shift Commander approval before \ncreating or activating the Department alert.  The officer shall document the alert \nentry in a case or supplemental report associated with the call for service. \n \n \nALPR ALERTS AND TRAFFIC STOPS \n \nAn ALPR alert shall not, by itself, establish reasonable suspicion or probable cause. \n \nBefore taking enforcement action based upon an ALPR alert, when circumstances \npermit, officers shall: \n \n• Visually verify that the license plate and other identifying characteristics \ncorrespond to the alert; \n• Confirm the status of the underlying record through COLLECT, NCIC, \nRMS, or another appropriate source; \n• Confirm, when practicable, that the underlying record remains active; and \n• Develop independent reasonable suspicion, probable cause, or other \nlegally sufficient grounds for the enforcement action. \n• Consider the totality of the circumstances.\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \nBefore proceeding with additional law enforcement action based on an ALPR hit, \nofficers shall receive confirmation from communications or other authorized personnel \nthat the hit remains active, unless exigent circumstances exist. \n \nPROHIBITED USES \n \nALPR systems and data shall not be used: \n \n• For personal purposes; \n \n• To harass, intimidate, retaliate against, or target an individual or group; \n \n• To monitor or investigate an individual based on actual or perceived race, \nethnicity, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, sex, pregnancy \nstatus, disability, citizenship, nationality, income level, or perceived criminal \nhistory, except when a prior conviction is an element of the offense being \ninvestigated; \n \n• To identify, monitor, investigate, or impose liability upon a person solely for \nconstitutionally protected speech, assembly, association, petitioning, or other \nFirst Amendment activity; \n \n• To investigate suspected immigration violations or otherwise assist in civil or \ncriminal immigration enforcement, except where expressly authorized by \ncontrolling law; \n \n• To investigate or prosecute an individual for seeking, receiving, providing, or \nassisting another person in obtaining reproductive health care services or \ngender-affirming health care services that are legal in Connecticut, or in any \nmanner that would violate Conn. Gen. Stat. 54-155b. \n \n• To circumvent Connecticut restrictions concerning public resources or law \nenforcement activities; \n \n• To obtain information for a purpose unrelated to an authorized ALPR use; \n \n• To provide unauthorized access to ALPR data; or \n \n• For any other purpose prohibited by federal or Connecticut law. \n \nDEPARTMENT & POST DISCIPLINARY ACTION  \n \nIf a law enforcement officer is found to have knowingly accessed, used, or disseminated \nALPR data with malicious intent for purposes beyond the act’s allowable uses, the \nALPR Administrator shall notify POSTC of suspected malicious misuse.  The officer is \nsubject to disciplinary action up to and including termination.  The officer may also be\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \nsubjected to POST disciplinary action, such as suspension, cancellation, or revocation \nof his or her POST certification. \n \nDEPLOYMENT STANDARDS \n \nBefore installation, each fixed ALPR device shall have written justification identifying the \ndeployment category, location-based rationale, data relied upon, and material \nlimitations.  The justification shall be retained for the life of the deployment.  Deployment \ncategories are gateway, corridor, targeted, and asset protection.  Deployment reviews \nshall consider travel-path exposure, not device count alone.  Targeted deployments \nshall have a defined review or sunset date and require written re-justification for \ncontinuation. \n \nThe Department shall maintain an established baseline for fixed ALPR deployments.  \nExpansion beyond the baseline shall be presented to the municipal legislative body, \npolice commission, or other designated committee, as applicable.  Fixed ALPR \nlocations shall be publicly posted when permitted by law. \n \nCOLLECTION RESTRICTIONS \n \nThe Department shall comply with all Connecticut statutory restrictions concerning \ncollection of ALPR data at or near protected facilities and organizations. \n \nUnless authorized pursuant to Conn. Gen. Stat. 29-6d, the Department shall not collect \nALPR data from a fixed ALPR system located on the premises of, or within 250 feet of, \na reproductive or sexual health facility that primarily provides gender-affirming health \ncare services permitted under Connecticut law, or a nonprofit or community organization \nthat primarily serves immigrant communities, when the facility or organization has \nnotified POSTC of its location. \n \n• A reproductive or sexual health facility that primarily provides gender-affirming \nhealth care services permitted under Connecticut law; or \n \n• A nonprofit or community organization that primarily serves immigrant \ncommunities, when the facility or organization has notified POSTC of its location \nand the statutory conditions for the restriction are satisfied. \n \nDATA SHARING \n \nThe Department shall not sell ALPR data. \n \nConsistent with POSTC requirements, the Department shall share or provide access to \nALPR data only to: \n \n1. A Connecticut public agency;\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \n2. An authorized law enforcement agency of Massachusetts, New York, or \nRhode Island, or a municipality of those jurisdictions; \n \n3. An eligible multijurisdictional task force of which a public agency of this state \nis a participating member; provided: \n \n• Such requesting law enforcement agency or task force provides a \nwritten declaration affirming that any data received will be used in \ncompliance with the prohibitions set forth below; \n \na) Will not be used for an immigration investigation or enforcement \naction. \nb) Will not be used to investigate or prosecute any individual who \nhas sought, received or provided reproductive health care \nservices or gender-affirming health care services that are \npermitted under the laws of this state. \nc) Will not be further disclosed except as permitted under the laws \nof this state. \n• In the case of a multijurisdictional task force, such specific data \nrequested must be approved by the head of such task force or such \nhead’s designee and is directly and reasonably relevant to a \nspecific investigation of such task force. \n \n4. Any other law enforcement agency other than those of this state or described \nabove, including any federal law enforcement agency, if such requesting law \nenforcement agency; \n \n• Has a judicially issued probable cause warrant for the specific data \nrequested, or \n• Is requesting specific data on an individual identified as a possible \nmatch in the Federal Terrorist Screening Database. \n \n5. An individual requesting data regarding a motor vehicle registered in such \nindividual’s name, provided if a motor vehicle has more than one owner, \nlessor or regular user; all such owners, lessors and regular users join in the \nrequest and are natural persons. \n \nThe Department shall not provide unrestricted, bulk, automatic, or real-time access to \nALPR data unless specifically authorized by law and consistent with required case-\nspecific documentation. \n \nRequests for ALPR data shall be reviewed by the ALPR Administrator or Chief of \nPolice/designee.\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \nWhere Connecticut law requires a written declaration, certification, warrant, or other \nlegal process from a requesting agency, the Department shall obtain and verify the \nrequired documentation before disclosure. \n \nWhen required, an authorized out-of-state recipient shall provide a written \ndeclaration affirming that the recipient will: \n \n• Use the information only for an authorized purpose; \n \n• Comply with applicable Connecticut law; \n \n• Not use the information for prohibited immigration enforcement; \n \n• Not use the information to investigate or prosecute prohibited reproductive or \ngender-affirming health care activity; \n \n• Not use the information for prohibited First Amendment investigations; \n \n• Not further disclose the information except as authorized by Connecticut law; and \n \n• Otherwise comply with the statutory restrictions governing the receipt and use of \nALPR data. \n \nThe Department shall maintain documentation of authorized data-sharing transactions. \n \nINTERSTATE AND MULTIJURISDICTIONAL SHARING \n \nInterstate and multijurisdictional sharing shall comply with the specific limitations \nestablished by Connecticut law. \n \nSharing with an authorized law enforcement agency from Massachusetts, New York, or \nRhode Island, or an authorized multijurisdictional task force, shall satisfy all statutory \nconditions before disclosure, including any required written declaration and, where \napplicable, task-force approval and case-specific relevance. \n \nSharing with another out-of-state or federal law enforcement agency shall not occur \nunless the statutory requirements for such disclosure are satisfied, including any \nrequired judicially issued probable-cause warrant or other statutory exception. \n \nThe Department shall not participate in or facilitate a sharing arrangement that \ncircumvents Connecticut law. \n \nIf the Department learns that a recipient has used ALPR data in a manner prohibited by \nConnecticut law, the Department shall take all action required by law, including \nnotification to POST or another governmental authority when required and suspension \nof further sharing where required.\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \n \nDATA-SHARING NETWORKS \n \nThe Department shall not participate in an ALPR data-sharing network unless the \nnetwork and its participating agencies comply with Connecticut law. \n \nThe Department shall maintain a written inventory identifying agency-owned and \nprivately owned devices from which it can access ALPR data including ownership.  \nQueries of third-party data shall be subject to the same justification, logging, and audit \nrequirements as Department-owned ALPR data.  The permissible purpose limit applies \nto the Department’s use, regardless of ownership. \n \nData received from private contributors shall be subject to the Department retention \npolicy upon receipt.  The municipal attorney shall be consulted regarding records and \ndisclosure requirements when necessary, and the conclusion documented. \n \nWhere required by law, participation shall be conditioned upon execution of a written \ndeclaration by participating entities affirming that ALPR data will be used and shared \nonly in compliance with Connecticut law. \n \nFor any multistate, intrastate, or national ALPR data-sharing system or network, \nparticipation shall also require the written declaration required by POSTC affirming that \nshared data will be used only in compliance with Connecticut law and will not be further \nshared except as permitted by law. \n \nThe Department shall not provide or permit access to ALPR data through a system that \nallows prohibited uses or disclosures. \n \nThe Department shall not permit real-time, bulk, or automatic access to ALPR data \nunless authorized by law, supported by a documented case-specific request, and \notherwise permitted under Connecticut law. \n \nDATA RETENTION \n \nA. General Retention Period \n \nBeginning October 1, 2026, the Department shall establish an ordinary ALPR \ndata retention period of seven (7) days. \n \nThe Department's 7-day retention period is intended to provide a more protective \nDepartment standard and shall be administered consistently with Connecticut \nlaw, including any statutory requirement for a shorter retention period under a \nvendor contract. \n \nThe Department shall not retain ALPR data merely because the information \nmight be useful in the future.\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \n \nOfficers may submit a case specific preservation request in writing to the Division \nCommander then approved by the Chief of Police outlining the need to preserve \ndata to the twenty-one (21) day state maximum statutory limit.  This request must \nbe approved by the Chief of Police to allow officers to retain the data.  ALPR data \nshall not be retained beyond 21 days unless a lawful exception applies. \n \nFor violent Class A or Class B felony matters, any extension of the ordinary \nretention period shall be in writing, state the articulable basis and why case-\nspecific preservation is inadequate, and be reported in the annual ALPR report \nand to the Division Commander. \n \nB. Statutory Exceptions \n \nNothing in the 21-day Department retention period shall prevent preservation or \nretention when required or expressly authorized by Connecticut law. \n \nStatutory exceptions include, where applicable: \n \na) Data retained pursuant to a warrant or court order; \nb) Data retained pursuant to court rules governing preservation of evidence; \nc) Data retained for highway usage fees, if applicable, subject to the statutory \ndeletion period; \nd) Data retained as evidence in an active criminal investigation or prosecution \nwhen the statutory requirements for extended retention are satisfied; \ne) Data retained to establish that a potential future offense, motor vehicle \nviolation, or infraction constitutes a subsequent offense for which the \napplicable penalty exceeds the penalty for the prior offense; \nf) Certain municipal motor vehicle tax-related data, when all statutory conditions \nare satisfied; and \ng) De-identified, aggregated, or summarized data used for authorized traffic \nanalytics. \n \nC. Active Criminal Investigations \n \nWhen ALPR data is designated for retention as evidence in an active criminal \ninvestigation or prosecution: \n \n1. A supervisory law enforcement officer shall approve the retention when \nrequired by law; \n2. The Department shall document the purpose for retention; \n3. The Department shall document the associated case number; and \n4. The Department shall document the date retention was authorized. \n5. The data shall be deleted when the applicable statutory termination event \noccurs unless another lawful basis requires continued preservation.\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \nD. Automatic Deletion \n \nThe Department and its ALPR vendor shall maintain procedures reasonably \ndesigned to automatically delete ALPR data when the applicable retention period \nexpires. \n \nThe Department shall periodically verify that automated deletion procedures are \nfunctioning properly. \n \nALPR RECORDS, LOGGING, AND QUARTERLY AUDITS \n \nThe Department or its vendor shall maintain access logs and audit information sufficient \nto ensure compliance with law and facilitate independent review. \n \nLogs shall include, when applicable: \n \n• The identity of the person accessing the system; \n• Date and time of access; \n• Query information; \n• The reason for access; \n• Associated case, complaint, or incident number; \n• Alert or hit information; \n• Data-sharing information; \n• Retention information; \n• The number of times ALPR data is retained beyond the ordinary retention period; \n• The duration of extended retention; and \n• Other information required by law, POSTC, or Department policy. \n \nThe Department shall conduct an ALPR audit monthly. \n \nEach monthly audit shall review, as applicable: \n \n• User access; \n• Query activity; \n• Reasons for access; \n• Required case or incident documentation; \n• Hot-list activity; \n• Data-sharing activity; \n• Retention and deletion activity; \n• Extended-retention decisions; \n• Vendor access; \n• Compliance with prohibited-use restrictions; \n• Security controls; and \n• Compliance with this policy and applicable law. \n• Audit deficiencies shall be documented.\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \n \nThe ALPR Administrator shall ensure that corrective action is taken when deficiencies \nare identified and shall report significant deficiencies or violations to the Chief of Police. \n \nAudit records shall be maintained in accordance with applicable law and Department \nrecords-retention requirements. \n \nAudit records shall be retained longer than the underlying ALPR data, with a minimum \nretention of (12) months when permitted by the applicable records-retention schedule, \nand shall be protected against unauthorized alteration or deletion.   \n \nWhere supported, automated exception reporting should identify missing query \njustification or case linkage, queries tied to closed incidents, queries by personnel not \nassigned to the incident, anomalous or repeat queries, exigent activity, bulk exports., \nand changes to configuration, retention, sharing permissions, or hot-list sources. \n \nSupervisors shall conduct monthly sampling of query and external-sharing activity.  The \nDepartment shall conduct an annual comprehensive ALPR review covering user \naccounts and roles, sharing, configuration and retention, hot-list sources, vendor \naccess, maintenance, and scheduled deletion. \n \nWhere practicable, the review shall be separate from the system operator.  Unexplained \naudit anomalies shall be documented and referred to the Chief of Police, Internal \nAffairs, or another appropriate authority when required by law or policy. \n \nACCOUNTABILITY AND POSTC SAFEGUARDS \n \nWhen ALPR data is used in connection with an incident or investigation, personnel shall \nclearly state in the applicable written report, affidavit, or court application that ALPR \ndata was used, unless otherwise authorized by the State’s Attorney, the Office of the \nInspector General, or the Chief’s State’s Attorney’s Office. \n \nALPR data shall not be queried in connection with any civil or criminal investigation \nconcerning reproductive health care, gender-affirming care, or any health care services \nthat are legal in Connecticut, or in any manner that would violate Conn. Gen. Stat. 54-\n155b. \n \nIf the Department suspects that an out-of-state or federal law enforcement agency has \nused shared ALPR data in a manner prohibited by the POSTC Model Policy or \nConnecticut law, the ALPR Administrator shall notify POSTC as required. \n \nFOIA AND PUBLIC RECORDS \n \nALPR data shall be handled in accordance with Connecticut's Freedom of Information \nAct and the specific confidentiality provisions applicable to ALPR information.\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \nALPR data is confidential and shall not be deemed a public record except as otherwise \nprovided by Connecticut law. \n \nThe following information shall be treated in accordance with applicable statutory public-\nrecord provisions, including: \n \n• Locations of ALPR recording devices, subject to statutory exceptions; and \n• Data derived from ALPR audits, usage logs, and logs detailing access to \nALPR data, with ALPR data redacted as required by law. \n \nRegistered Vehicle Information Requests: \n \nRequests for ALPR information concerning a motor vehicle registered in the requester's \nname shall be processed in accordance with applicable Connecticut law. Where the \nvehicle is registered to more than one individual, all required owners, lessors, or regular \nusers shall participate in or consent to the request as required by law. \n \nAll FOIA requests involving ALPR information shall be referred to the Chief of Police or \ndesignee and processed in consultation with the appropriate municipal legal authority \nwhen necessary. \n \nEVIDENCE AND PRESERVATION \n \nALPR data that has evidentiary value shall be preserved in accordance with applicable \nlaw, Department evidence procedures, court rules, and applicable retention \nrequirements. \n \nWhen ALPR data is preserved as evidence, personnel shall: \n \n• Document the reason for preservation; \n• Associate the information with the appropriate case or incident; \n• Preserve the information in an approved secure location; \n• Maintain appropriate chain-of-custody documentation; and \n• Ensure that preservation does not result in retention beyond the period \nauthorized by law unless a lawful exception applies. \n• A preservation request, warrant, court order, litigation hold, or other lawful \npreservation requirement shall be promptly referred to a supervisor and, where \nappropriate, the municipal attorney or other authorized legal representative. \n \n \n \nDATA SECURITY \n \nALPR information shall be protected through appropriate administrative, technical, and \nphysical safeguards.\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \nAt a minimum: \n \n• Access shall be individually assigned; \n• Shared user credentials are prohibited; \n• ALPR systems shall only be accessed from a department issued computer which \ncontains the multi-factor authentication. \n• Access will be limited to the officers role and department needs; \n• Unauthorized copying or downloading is prohibited; \n• ALPR data shall be stored only in approved systems; \n• Electronic transfers shall use appropriate security controls; \n• Vendor access shall be limited to authorized purposes; \n• System sessions shall automatically terminate after a defined period of inactivity; \n• Security incidents shall be reported promptly; and \n• The Department shall comply with applicable CJIS requirements when ALPR \ninformation contains or is derived from criminal justice information. \n \nVENDOR REQUIREMENTS \n \nAny private vendor, contractor, or third-party entity that operates an ALPR system or \nstores, processes, transmits, or provides access to ALPR data on behalf of the \nDepartment shall comply with applicable Connecticut law, this policy, and contractual \nrequirements. \n \nALPR-related contracts shall, as applicable and required by law: \n \n• Prohibit the vendor from selling ALPR data; \n• Restrict vendor sharing or disclosure of ALPR data; \n• Prohibit unauthorized access by third parties; \n• Require appropriate information-security safeguards; \n• Require industry-recognized security practices, including encryption in transit and \nat rest. \n• Prohibited commingling Department ALPR data with data held or stored for \nanother public agency or person; \n• Require compliance with Connecticut law; \n• Establish retention and deletion requirements; \n• Require cooperation with lawful audits and investigations; \n• Require vendor access to be time-stamped, attributed to a named individual, \nlogged, and reported to the Department; \n• Address data-breach and security-incident obligations; \n• Require preservation of data when legally required; \n• Require return or deletion of Department data upon contract termination when \nappropriate; \n• Establish applicable jurisdiction and governing-law requirements; and \n• Require the vendor to consent to personal jurisdiction in Connecticut and provide \nthat Connecticut law governs the contract;\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \n• Establish applicable jurisdiction and governing-law requirements; and \n• Incorporate applicable statutory restrictions concerning prohibited uses and \ndisclosures. \n \nNo vendor terms of service, end-user license agreement, or other contract language \nshall authorize conduct prohibited by Connecticut law or this policy. \n \nThe Department shall periodically review vendor compliance. \n \nDATA SECURITY INCIDENTS AND BREACHES \n \nAny suspected unauthorized access, acquisition, disclosure, loss, alteration, or \ncompromise of ALPR information shall be reported immediately to the ALPR \nAdministrator and Chief of Police or designee. \n \nThe Department shall comply with applicable Connecticut data-security and breach-\nnotification requirements. \n \nWhere ALPR data is subject to additional statutory, contractual, CJIS, or other security \nrequirements, those requirements shall also apply. \n \nThe Department shall document the incident and take appropriate corrective action. \n \nTRAINING \n \nPersonnel shall complete standardized ALPR training before receiving access and shall \ncomplete periodic training as required by law, POSTC, or Department policy. \n \nTraining shall include: \n \n• System access and security controls; \n• Hardware and software operation; \n• Query procedures; \n• Hot-list management; \n• Alert verification; \n• Traffic-stop procedures; \n• Data retention and deletion; \n• Evidence preservation; \n• Data-sharing restrictions; \n• Interstate and multijurisdictional sharing; \n• Privacy and civil liberties; \n• First Amendment protections; \n• Prohibited uses; \n• Connecticut restrictions concerning immigration enforcement;\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \n• Connecticut restrictions concerning reproductive and gender-affirming health \ncare; \n• Protected-characteristic restrictions; \n• Vendor requirements; \n• FOIA and public-record requirements; \n• Security incident and breach reporting; \n• Quarterly audit and accountability requirements; \n• Consequences of misuse; and \n• Changes to applicable statutes, regulations, POSTC requirements, DESPP \nrequirements, and Department policy. \n• Training shall be updated whenever significant changes occur in applicable law, \nregulation, POSTC policy, system capabilities, or Department procedures. \n \nCHIEF OF POLICE RESPONSIBILITIES \n \nThe Chief of Police shall: \n \n• Designate the ALPR Administrator; \n• Ensure appropriate system oversight; \n• Ensure appropriate data-security measures; \n• Ensure compliance with applicable retention requirements; \n• Ensure ALPR audits are conducted at least monthly; \n• Ensure audit deficiencies are documented and corrected; \n• Approve or designate approval authority for applicable external data-sharing \narrangements; \n• Ensure required ALPR usage reports are completed and submitted; \n• Ensure required reports, certifications, and declarations are maintained; \n• Ensure employees receive required initial and periodic training; \n• Ensure the Department maintains an accurate inventory of ALPR systems and \nlocations; \n• Ensure vendor contracts contain required protections; \n• Ensure the policy is reviewed periodically; \n• Ensure policy revisions are made when necessary to maintain compliance with \nConnecticut law, POSTC requirements, and DESPP requirements; and \n• Ensure significant violations or compliance concerns are addressed \nappropriately. \n \nSUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES \n \nSupervisors shall: \n \n• Ensure personnel use ALPR systems in accordance with this policy; \n• Approve hot-list entries when required; \n• Review requests for extended data retention when required; \n• Ensure evidence is properly preserved;\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \n• Address suspected misuse; \n• Ensure required documentation is completed; \n• Ensure personnel comply with applicable access and query requirements; and \n• Report violations to the ALPR Administrator or Chief of Police. \n \nTRANSPARENCY AND ANNUAL REPORTING \n \nThe Department shall publish this ALPR policy on its Internet website or another \nappropriate public platform as required by Connecticut law or POSTC requirements. \n \nBeginning with the reporting period required by Connecticut law, the Department shall \ncomplete and submit the standardized ALPR usage report required by POSTC not later \nthan January 31 following each calendar year in which the Department used an ALPR \nsystem.  The Department shall submit the standardized ALPR usage report required by \nPOSTC to the Institute for Municipal and Regional Policy at the University of \nConnecticut and shall publish the report on the Department’s website. \n \nThe annual report shall contain the information required by the POSTC standardized \nreporting form, which may include: \n \n• Number of license plates scanned; \n• Number of searches performed and the reason for each search; \n• Number of times ALPR data was shared with or accessed by another entity; \n• Identity of entities receiving or accessing the data; \n• Reasons for sharing; \n• Number of times data was shared or accessed pursuant to judicial warrant; \n• Instances in which data was retained longer than otherwise permissible; \n• Requests to collect data at protected locations; \n• Times data was accessed or requested after the statutory period specified by \nlaw; and \n• Changes to Department ALPR collection, retention, or sharing policies affecting \nprivacy. \n \nREQUIRED DOCUMENTATION AND RECORDS \n \nThe Department shall maintain documentation required by Connecticut law, POSTC \nrequirements, Department policy, and applicable contracts, including as applicable: \n \n• ALPR system locations; \n• Device deployment justifications and deployment category; \n• Mobile ALPR equipment; \n• Authorized users; \n• Training records; \n• Queries and access logs; \n• Hot-list entries;\n\n\"A Police-Community Partnership Since 1938\" \n• Data-sharing requests and approvals; \n• Required written declarations or certifications; \n• Warrants or other legal process supporting data sharing or access when \nrequired; \n• Extended-retention approvals; \n• Preservation requests; \n• Quarterly audit reports; \n• Corrective-action documentation; \n• Security incidents; \n• Vendor compliance documentation; \n• Annual ALPR usage reports; and \n• Other records concerning ALPR system usage required by law or Department \npolicy. \n \nPERIODIC REVIEW AND LEGAL UPDATE \n \nThe Chief of Police or designee shall review this policy periodically and whenever \nnecessary to ensure continued compliance with Connecticut statutes; applicable federal \nlaw; POSTC requirements; DESPP regulations; Applicable court decisions; Changes in \nALPR technology; and Department operational requirements.","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":3422.4052119998305,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}